WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.180
Speaker 1: The governor of Oregon has commuted&nbsp;
all death sentences for the 17 individuals on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:15.540
death row in Oregon. The governor is against&nbsp;
the death penalty. And I think that this is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:22.920
absolutely fantastic decision. CNN reports&nbsp;
Oregon governor calls death penalty immoral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:29.100
commute sentences for all 17 inmates on death on&nbsp;
death row. Outgoing Oregon Governor Kate Brown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:36.720
is commuting the sentences of all 17 people&nbsp;
on death row to life imprisonment without the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:42.420
possibility of parole, according to a news release&nbsp;
yesterday. She said since taking office in 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:48.000
I have continued Oregon's moratorium on executions&nbsp;
because the death penalty is both dysfunctional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.300
and immoral. Today I am commuting Oregon's death&nbsp;
row so that we will no longer have anyone serving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:01.260
a sentence of death and facing execution&nbsp;
in this state. She used executive clemency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:06.420
powers to commute the sentences. It is set to&nbsp;
take effect today. Now, important to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:12.120
about the death penalty in Oregon generally,&nbsp;
as the article says, Oregon has executed two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:19.560
people since the US Supreme Court reinstated the&nbsp;
death penalty in 1976 and since Oregon reinstated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:27.600
the death penalty in 1984. The most recent was in&nbsp;
1997. I think that this is a fantastic decision.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:34.740
I'm against the death penalty. I believe we should&nbsp;
all be against the death penalty. And there are so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:40.860
many reasons, some of which I will tell you right&nbsp;
now. Now, I want to say upfront, sometimes there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:45.780
are people who are against the death penalty for&nbsp;
three of the reasons I'm going to mention. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:49.560
they will say, David, that fourth reason isn't&nbsp;
very good. It's sort of fallacious, actually.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.540
Let's take them one at a time. And I know&nbsp;
specifically what people are talking about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:59.340
but let's take them one at a time. First&nbsp;
of all, if we believe that human lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.440
have inherent value, which is often part&nbsp;
of the justification for the death penalty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:10.920
meaning it goes something like human life has&nbsp;
inherent value. And so when you take a life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:18.060
you are giving up your right to live and&nbsp;
therefore the state can or should take your life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:24.180
intentionally. If we are against the intentional&nbsp;
taking of lives which have inherent value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:31.860
then we can't say except when the person has&nbsp;
done A, B, or C, at which point the state should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.420
get involved in the business of taking life.&nbsp;
Nope, doesn't make sense. The state should not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:44.340
be involved in that and I am against it. Secondly,&nbsp;
there are wrongful convictions all the time in our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:50.040
justice system, and in addition to there being&nbsp;
innocent people on death row, there are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:58.320
many cognitively limited people on death row.&nbsp;
And so because we can't possibly get it right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:04.860
we can't accept as a society the possibility of&nbsp;
putting to death someone who may be innocent. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.360
you could say. But, David, what if the system&nbsp;
were perfect and there were no one innocent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.640
I would still be against it for the other reasons&nbsp;
that I'm mentioning. So don't. Just because one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:22.320
reason alone isn't bulletproof. Don't say it's not&nbsp;
a reason. The state shouldn't be taking lives. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.940
there are innocent people on death row and there&nbsp;
are cognitively limited people on death row.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:33.120
Solving just one of those things doesn't actually&nbsp;
change anything. You can't undo taking a life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.400
whereas if you were in prison, someone and they're&nbsp;
innocent, there's at least the chance of fixing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.860
it and compensating them in the future. Third,&nbsp;
it does not appear. Appear as though the death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:49.800
penalty is an effective deterrent to crime and&nbsp;
that it is not actually making society safer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:56.220
It's it's beyond the scope of this segment to&nbsp;
look at that. But it's been widely studied.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:01.680
And so if it's not a deterrent, then why are we&nbsp;
putting the state in the position of obtaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:06.300
the chemicals that we use, which sometimes work&nbsp;
and sometimes take hours? It doesn't make any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:14.220
sense. I also don't think that the death penalty&nbsp;
is the harshest sentence for the offender. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:20.700
what I mean by that is based on my beliefs, you&nbsp;
put someone to death and the lights go out and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:26.280
they're suffering or living with the consequences&nbsp;
of their actions ends. If you put someone in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:31.560
prison without the possibility of parole, they're&nbsp;
going to sit there for however long they live,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:39.300
living with the reality of what they did and that&nbsp;
they now live in a box forever. Now, I am not one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:45.240
to say that there should be extra punishments,&nbsp;
like, for example, prison rape. I'm against that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:51.000
Those are indications that our prison system is&nbsp;
unable to keep inmates safe. But I do believe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:57.360
turning the lights off, for lack of a better term,&nbsp;
is a less harsh punishment than making someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:03.480
sit in prison for the rest of their life. So if&nbsp;
your desire is the worst possible punishment and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:09.060
experience for the offender, it's life in prison&nbsp;
without parole. And then if your concerns are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:14.640
financial, if you care about money, it's cheaper&nbsp;
to do prison without the possibility of parole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:20.700
because in our system, individuals on death&nbsp;
row have significant access to appeals. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.600
some people will say, get rid of the appeals. No,&nbsp;
no, no. We need those appeals because there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:30.360
so many innocent people on death row. So there&nbsp;
is no reason to be for the death penalty. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.740
I think what Kate Brown is doing is absolutely&nbsp;
fantastic and I'm glad to see that she did it.

